  geopolitics: "The transmission chain from this war to the FX board ran one way all last week and reversed over the weekend, which is the single most important thing to carry into Monday's open. The week's move was a political repricing as much as a diplomatic one: Brent fell more than 8% on Wednesday alone, to a two-month low near 94.62, with Reuters tying it to expectations that a Democratic sweep of both chambers curtails the administration's capacity to escalate and to impose tariffs, and Qatari mediation drained what was left of the premium on top. The closes confirm the scale - WTI 90.830 and minus 12.76% on the week, Brent 98.320 and minus 13.32%, gasoline 2.7767 and minus 12.03%, euro-area gas 85.59 and minus 17.66%, UK gas 212.25 and minus 17.60%, German gas 87.04 and minus 17.40%. Then the weekend. The IRGC reported tanker strikes on three consecutive days: five oil tankers and a tugboat ablaze in or near the Strait of Hormuz on November 5, two more hitting mines on November 6, two more exploding and burning on November 7. Trump has said the United States will destroy Iranian vessels laying further mines. Washington widened its naval blockade of Iran's Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman ports to add a task force aimed at disrupting ground transport, and disabled another ship attempting to breach it on November 7. Tehran's position has not moved: Hormuz stays closed until seven conditions are met, among them waiving oil sanctions, ending the blockade and releasing frozen funds. UKMTO has attacks running near a record pace and flows remain well below capacity. Qatari mediation is genuinely live - both sides say they are committed to it, and there is a reported plan to cut the memorandum's preliminary negotiation window from 60 days to two, with Iranian officials expected in Doha around November 7 - but an Iranian official describes the two as still far apart, and the widely-cited report that Washington approved the Qatari draft memorandum is single-sourced and unconfirmed. It should not be traded as fact. The sharpest point is a conditional that has quietly expired: the restraint Trump offered was that the United States would not launch new attacks before the vote, and the vote has happened. So the market has priced out a war premium on the strength of midterm gridlock in the same week that the physical war intensified and the one explicit constraint on escalation lapsed. One detail cuts against reading the whole collapse as a risk-premium unwind: Urals fell 10.00% on Friday alone and 25.34% on the week, far more than WTI's 12.76%, and a discount widening that fast reads as sanctioned-barrel displacement rather than global demand destruction - which is less bearish for North American crude than the headline move suggests. The FX read is therefore about position quality rather than direction. First, the honest expression of the asymmetry is not an oil long: the complex has whipsawed three times in five sessions, which is why USOIL is a range with the upside skew named rather than a bias. Second, every currency whose central bank built a hawkish case on this input is exposed to the deflation and would be rescued by an escalation - the euro, sterling, the Aussie, the Kiwi and the Canadian dollar all qualify, and the Canadian dollar most directly, which is why USD/CAD comes down from high to medium conviction and rests on labour data rather than on crude. Third, the dollar is the one leg that needs neither outcome: the Fed votes on December 9 without ever seeing the energy collapse in a CPI print, because Tuesday's covers October and November's arrives December 10. Fourth, the haven read favours the franc over the yen on the week's own evidence - the franc was the only currency to gain against the dollar at plus 0.56% while the yen lost ground to the franc, the euro and sterling - and CHF/JPY is both the cleanest expression of that and the live test of it, because an escalation that finally bids the yen is precisely what would prove the yen call wrong.",
